Drive the filter pill bar with the pointer.

The bar was reachable by Tab and Shift+Tab and by nothing else, which is a
strange thing for a row of buttons sitting under the cursor. A horizontal
swipe now walks it and a click jumps straight to a capsule; clicking the one
already filtering clears the filter, so the same gesture both engages and
undoes it.

Swiping returns Tab's own keys rather than moving pill_active itself, so
"the next pill" keeps one implementation, wraparound and BM_PILL_NONE start
included. Clicking cannot go through a key, since no key names a particular
capsule, so it writes pill_active directly and the client picks it up through
bm_menu_get_active_pill() the same way it picks up Tab's move.

Hit-testing uses the edges the painter last drew, recorded per capsule as it
lays the run out, rather than re-deriving them. The run has a scroll offset
applied and both ends clipped, and computing that twice would let the hit test
and the picture disagree about where a capsule is. Capsules the painter
skipped keep zeroed edges, which is what stops one scrolled out of sight from
swallowing a click at the left margin.

Consumed in poll_key() rather than through the bm_pointer path, because
beamenu drives its loop with bm_menu_run_with_key() and never calls
poll_pointer(). Handling it there also lands the result in the same turn: the
client re-reads the active pill after every run_with_key(), so a pill moved
now filters immediately, where one moved during the next render() would sit
unnoticed until the user pressed something.

bm_pills_hit() joins bm_pills_scroll_offset() in lib/renderers/pills.cpp and
is covered by the same ASan/UBSan driver.

